  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between amoxapine and Sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG) through a chain of side effects. Amoxapine is associated with parotid swelling, which is a side effect of cyclobenzaprine and trimipramine, both of which have SHBG as their target. Similarly, amoxapine is linked to lymphadenitis, a side effect of trimipramine, which also targets SHBG. These findings indicate that amoxapine may influence SHBG through its side effects, although the direct mechanism remains unclear. The scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of support for this relationship, but the evidence is not definitive. The integration of these paths highlights the possibility that amoxapine could modulate SHBG activity via its side effects, though further research is needed to establish a causal link.",
